From d5d8da65cd97e11d1bc3406c1ce5f7c35e76930f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: benmandrew <benmandrew@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 21:47:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(data): import and summarise the PSP campaign

The PSP campaign (peredur experiments/2026-11-20-psp-order) had no
path into the draft. Its results, seeded corpus, pooled pass and
trivial-reversion probe were not imported, and summarise.py did not
reduce them, so every PSP number would have to be typed by hand, which
the data rules forbid.

import_data.py gains import_psp(). It copies the campaign's CSVs from
a peredur checkout (~/projects/peredur-psp by default; --psp DIR or
$PEREDUR_PSP overrides it). --psp-only imports nothing else. A file
absent from the checkout keeps data/'s copy. No PSP data is committed.

summarise.py gains psp(), which writes psp-* levels:

- found-repair and known-repair rates, with McNemar and a wall-time
  Hodges-Lehmann shift against base and against full, and Holm over
  four features (novelty is one of them)
- the seeded corpus, through seeded_levels and clustered by base
  system, with a headline subset taken from the probe
- pooled measures for the specifications and the seeded subjects

To share code, pooled_share and pooled_reach take a pairs argument and
seeded_levels takes a features argument. The defaults keep the old
behaviour.

Interval recording stops after interval_check, so the PSP intervals do
not change the counts it reports.

Tested. With no PSP files, stats.json and stats.tex are byte-identical
to before. With the 2026-11-20 smoke runs (6 runs on psp-crossing, 12
on two seeded subjects, a 2-subject probe) and synthetic pooled CSVs,
in a scratch copy of data/, the non-PSP keys did not change and 32
psp-* levels appeared.
